  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe surface trough won't budge much today, and we'll see \ncoverage of showers increase again through the day. An upper \ntrough will approach from the west and begin to slow down and \nclose off into a low. More of a scattered shower type look today\nrather than a more coherent shield of rain. The column dries a \nbit in the mid and upper levels, but showers will still be \nproductive with the bulk of the moisture in the warm cloud \nlayer. Still not concerned about hourly rates as the 10 km \nneighborhood probability for 1\"/hour in any given hour is 10% or\nless. 24 hour mean totals from HREF through 8am Wednesday range\nfrom a quarter to half of an inch, and even the 90th percentile\npeaks around 0.75-1.25 inches for parts of northern West \nVirginia. Overflow/areal flooding threats remain, thus, the \nFlood Watch continues through 12Z Wednesday for most of northern\nWest Virginia and the southwestern PA ridges.\n\nSupport for rain will gradually decrease through Wednesday as \nthe surface low continues shifting south. An inverted trough\nwill set-up Wednesday night extending northward into \nsouthwestern Ohio bringing the chance for rain west. Longer \nrange ensembles then point toward a dry weekend locally as a \ncoastal low develops off the Mid-Atlantic and shortwave ridging\nbetween that and a trough across the Dakotas becomes our \ndominant feature.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBeneath the cold upper low, with persistent easterly flow, and \nbeneath thick cloud cover and showers, NBM advertises a 60-70% \nchance that the PA & WV ridges can't get above 60F for highs on\nWednesday. Elsewhere, the goal posts spread from low 60s on the\ncool end to mid 60s on the warm end, and given the conditions \nin place, it's likely a better bet to hedge cooler. Minimum \ntemperatures will drop 5-10 degrees below normal Friday and\nSaturday morning. So, even optimistically, mid 60s is likely \nour ceiling through Thursday before more seasonable temperatures\nreturn for the weekend.",
